why 3 1/2 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and tenor go from a twelfth to a fifth in opposite directions. Strict exercises forbid this too, but the contrary motion keeps the two lines apart.
why 1 0 RANGE The bass starts high, stepping E♭–D–C–B♭ in step with the opening of the tune. Bach begins in a light, high register and only later takes the bass down.
why 1 1/8 RANGE Still the high start: the bass steps down D–C–B♭ toward its usual range.
why 3 0 SPACING The soprano climbs to its high F while the alto sits low on D. The alto climbs E♭–F–G over the next beats and the gap closes.
why 3 1/8 SPACING The same gap; the alto is climbing toward the soprano.
why 4 1/2 OVERLAP The alto drops a fifth to C at the fermata, below the tenor's last note E. The tenor leaps down to A at the same moment, so the voices stay in order.
why 4 1/2 LEADING_TONE Instead of rising to F, the tenor's E leaps down to A, the third of the chord. Soprano and alto have C and the bass F, so without the tenor's A there would be no third.
why 7 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All four voices rise as the music turns toward C minor. The bass's B♮ is a leading tone heading for C, and the soprano's climb E♭–F is the tune.
why 9 1/4 DOUBLING Alto and tenor pass through A at the same moment in contrary scales, the alto rising and the tenor falling. A passing doubling inside one dominant chord.
why 9 3/8 SPACING The alto climbs and the tenor falls in contrary scales, so the gap between them widens for a few eighths. It closes again by the end of the bar.
why 9 1/2 SPACING The contrary scales reach their widest point, alto up at C and tenor down at F. Both turn back at once.
why 9 1/2 CROSSING The tenor's scale runs down to F, under the bass's A, while the bass circles A–G–F–G–A. The lines cross briefly inside one held dominant chord.
why 9 5/8 SPACING The gap starts to close: the alto turns down to B♭ and the tenor climbs back to G.
why 9 5/8 CROSSING The tenor climbs back G–A above the bass, which drops to F; by the next beat the order is normal again.
why 11 1/4 SPACING The tenor drops low, E♭–F–G, to move with the bass while the alto rises with the melody. The open space lies between alto and tenor.
why 11 3/8 SPACING The same open spacing: alto climbing with the soprano, tenor low with the bass.
why 11 1/2 SPACING Still open: tenor and bass move together low while the upper pair moves high.
why 11 5/8 SPACING The tenor climbs G–A toward the alto, and the gap closes by the next beat.
why 11 3/4 DOUBLING Alto (B♭–A) and tenor (G–A) meet on the leading tone by step. The alto's A passes on down to G; the tenor's A rises to B♭ as it should.
